  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warmer and more humidity during the second half \nof the week. \n\n930 PM Update...\nLow clouds pretty much gone with only high clouds streaming\noverhead. This should remain the same overnight. Without the low\nclouds, we will have some fog form overnight, mainly in the\nvalleys. Dewpoint depressions already down to 2 at BFD, 1 at \nSEG and 1 at THV as they race each other to the bottom of the \ntemperature rankings.\n\nPrevious discussion...\nCooler temperatures through the middle of the week associated \nwith a trough will give way to increasing humidity and warmer \ntemperatures starting Wednesday afternoon. Increased \ntemperatures and humidity will last through the start of next \nweek. \n\nTemperatures from earlier in the week in the 70s will warm to the \nupper 70s to mid 80s later in the week and into the weekend. \nSoutherly return flow on the backside of a departing sfc high will \nhelp induce WAA and start to push increased moisture into the \nregion. A passing warm front later Wednesday into Thursday will \nfurther increase temperatures and moisture across the area as the \nwarm sector of a passing sfc low becomes established over the \nregion. The cold front associated with the system will cross the \narea late Thursday into Friday morning. A drop in temperatures by a \nfew degrees for Friday/Saturday will be possible behind the front. \nBut temperatures and humidity will rebound and increase once\nagain. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers with strong to severe thunderstorms are \nexpected for Thursday. \n\nA pre-frontal trough tracking across the area ahead of the \napproaching cold front will cross the area during the afternoon \nhours. Currently the outlook from SPC is for a Marginal Risk for \nsevere thunderstorms across the entire area. Current guidance brings \nsomewhat favorable shear values of around 30 knots along with \ninstability of around 1,000-1,500 J/kg. Uncertainty among guidance \nfor the amount of cloud cover still remains as of the most recent \nmodel runs. Damaging winds with a lower chance for hail remains the \nprimary hazard. \n\nIncreased PWat values to around 1.50\" will also help increase the \npotential for heavy rainfall with thunderstorms that develop. The \neastern half of the area still remain in a Marginal Risk for \nexcessive runoff from WPC.\n\nThursday night continues to look poor for lunar eclipse viewing\n(around midnight local), especially for the eastern half of PA.\nClouds and showers may not clear off by that time for the east.\nBut, there is a chance for the west to begin to clear out.\nHowever, low clouds may also start to form in the mountains. So,\nthe outlook for a clear or partly cloudy sky is not great.",
